[Music]  happy birthday tasty it's crazy that  you're turning five it's been such an  awesome and fun journey i'm excited to  see where this takes us next  [Music]  hey it's alvin and we're celebrating  tasty's fifth birthday and i'm gonna be  sharing stories about you know moments  and favorite videos from each of those  five years  taking it all the way back to 2015. five  years ago my approach to videos pretty  much the same as the way i make food  videos today i just wanted to make  recipes that i thought were fun and i  thought other people could find fun from  2015 the one recipe that really stands  out to me was the white chocolate magic  ball this idea was inspired by a dessert  that was going viral on instagram so i  set out to recreate it this one was  probably  one of my trickiest recipes because i  had to find a mold that would make sense  it was a journey to figure out that we  needed to use clear plastic ornaments  usually found on christmas trees it was  really a kind of recreate from scratch  moment because there wasn't a lot of  information  on the actual recipe served at the  restaurant  at the time i wasn't making a lot of  desserts so that was completely new i'm  not like a pastry chef or a chocolatier  filming the video is also pretty tricky  because the chocolate melts pretty  quickly  and there's like a little window where  you have to pour it but get the right  collapse from the chocolate ball and i'm  pretty sure i shot that part like 12  times a hole is cut into the bottom and  you can fill it with whatever you like i  chose brownies and fruit and ice cream  once that is all plated a really hot  chocolate ganache or sauce is poured on  top of the cold white chocolate sphere  and the whole thing melts away kind of  like magic  [Music]  2016 tasty was picking up in popularity  food videos were kind of taking over the  internet and it was a really exciting  time one of my favorite recipes in 2016  was spinach dip mozzarella sticks i just  took two things i love to eat at parties  and put them together so for this recipe  it starts off pretty basic we're gonna  make a normal spinach dip with cream  cheese spinach all those nice cheeses  and seasonings and the cool part is that  this spinach tip has a lot more  mozzarella added into the actual dip  itself because after that the mixture  gets spread on a parchment paper it gets  frozen and then cut into rectangular  sticks that are then breaded and then  fried and when it fries the cheese  inside that we added before  melts so that when it pulls apart it's  almost like a mozzarella stick but  there's also spinach dip perfect party  food  2017  one of my favorite recipes this year was  the jiggly fluffy japanese cheesecake  it's so fluffy oh my god i actually got  the chance to go to japan with rye a  fellow tasty producer we work with the  tasty japan team one popular dish was  this jiggly cheesecake that was that is  probably really well known now but back  then it was like this instagram viral  thing that only a couple of shops were  selling this one is probably  my second hardest video on tasty i've  ever made i made about 15 of these  before i actually figured out the  correct ratios essentially this recipe  is a kind of egg yolk cheesecake batter  that is folded into a bunch of egg  whites so in the beginning it's a lot of  egg yolks it's some cream cheese milk  sugar flour all that nice stuff and that  gets set aside and what happens next is  kind of the core of the cheesecake which  is like a ton of egg whites because the  whole thing what makes it jiggly is the  air that is trapped inside the whipped  egg whites when it bakes so the whole  cheesecake is highly dependent on really  well whipped egg whites once those are  done the main batter gets folded in and  it gets poured into a large cake pan  that is baked in a water bath for a very  very very long time it's essentially a  giant souffle cake this recipe i think  is pretty satisfying in a lot of  different ways you have the peeling of  the paper you have the way it jiggles  when you set it down and kind of wobbles  around a bit and then the powdered sugar  on top and overall it just looks very  aesthetically pleasing which is why i  think it's very popular and it also  tastes pretty awesome too  [Music]  2018. at this point tasty is expanding  we're trying a lot of different things  we're trying shows we have a lot of  longer form content on youtube and it  was a great time to just try a lot of  cool things i've always had a  fascination for really visual foods so  i decided to make a giant version of a  burger came down to really figuring out  how to make a giant bun a giant slice of  cheese how to make two giant patties the  lettuce and the tomatoes so for me it  was almost like this fun architecture  experiment and seeing you know what  recipes were out there but how that  looked like if i multiplied it by 10 or  20. so it was a cool process to go  through where i literally had to figure  out what the biggest pan i had was how  big the oven was what the cook times  were and the different solves that you  deal with when food is that large and i  think that was the biggest challenge but  also the most fun part of the experience  so people were into that video and after  that i was lucky enough to be able to  produce a whole season of making it big  where you go around and make giant  versions of people's favorite foods like  ramen chicken nuggets sushi pizza so  that was a really fun time  2019.  i was experimenting a lot more with  different kinds of videos i wanted to  showcase a lot of chefs instead of doing  everything all the cooking myself so i  went out and shot a lot of videos that  are almost documentary inspired  with a lot of chefs i did a couple ones  with a mozzarella master a chinese hand  poodle noodle master a japanese soba  master  and i will probably say my favorite one  that i've ever produced was the one with  a sushi master i wanted to showcase what  it was like to be one of the elite  craftsmen in his field not just the way  he cooks but also the the parts that  lead up to it that a lot of people don't  see the most interesting thing is that  he is only open for dinner for 16 people  every night that's the maximum but  there's so much work that goes on in the  morning late night even the day before  that i think was really important to  show shooting that video was an  experience as well his  whole restaurant was essentially like  this temple we made sure not to bump  into anything you know all the wood was  imported from japan we had to be very  careful with our camera equipment we  didn't want to intrude on his work we  just wanted to sit there capture  everything silently the way he would do  it normally and it was a really cool  balance to not only get the chance to  film a master like him but to appreciate  his work through the lens of a camera  and 2020 this year we really wanted to  show that  you can do so much with cooking at home  and this is the first time i really  focused on giving glimpses into the  actual people behind the camera and the  recipes to show how they actually cook  at home  i've been a fan of a lot of korean and  japanese youtube food craters they have  pioneered the style of a food cinematic  vlog that uses subtitles and music and  the sounds of cooking to kind of make a  video very relaxing i'm i was really  inspired by those videos i think cooking  with three ingredients  is really fun because there's this  challenge of making a dish that people  might know but with way less ingredients  so it automatically makes things more  accessible to new cooks who might be  trying recipes or cooking for the first  time and it also is easier since you  don't have to go grocery shopping for 20  bajillion things and this is kind of  magic science behind why certain three  ingredients um can come together to  create something that usually would take  20. so i like that part of it a lot in  this video i also took inspiration from  a korean trend which is the whipped  talagona coffee  i made a fluffy souffle jiggly omelette  again with a lot of asian influence i'm  a teriyaki chicken the way i would  usually make it for myself to eat on a  daily basis  and i made creme brulee which i used a  trick from my friend rye who uses melted  ice cream  to substitute for all the eggs and the  sugar so it was a fun  food packed day but not that many  ingredients  [Music]  so there you have it those are some of  my favorite videos from my five years  at tasty happy birthday tasty and here's  the five more  oh yes  [Music]  you

